  {
   "x": 33,
   "y": 10,
   "text": "FUNGIE. A bottlenose dolphin who turned up in Dingle harbour in 1983 and greeted the boats for over thirty-five years. In 2020 he swam off, and the whole town went quiet. The statue is here so we've somewhere to say hello."
  },
  {
   "x": 29,
   "y": 9,
   "text": "DINGLE, AN DAINGEAN. 'The fortress', in Irish. About two thousand people, a Gaeltacht town where Irish is still spoken, and a lot of pubs, some of which are also hardware shops.",
   "local": {
    "ga": "An Daingean"
   }
  },
  {
   "x": 50,
   "y": 26,
   "text": "BUS STOP. Tralee, Killarney, Slea Head Drive. The timetable says 'approximately'. It's an honest timetable."
  },
  {
   "x": 20,
   "y": 27,
   "text": "SLEA HEAD DRIVE. A thirty-mile loop round the end of the peninsula, with the Blasket Islands out to sea and a view that makes people stop their cars in the middle of the road. Please don't."
  },
  {
   "x": 43,
   "y": 33,
   "text": "THE COVE. Grey seals haul out on the rocks below the harbour. They know exactly how photogenic they are."
  },
  {
   "x": 48,
   "y": 14,
   "text": "BLUE PLAQUE: Tom Crean, Antarctic explorer with Scott and Shackleton, was born at Annascaul on the Dingle Peninsula in 1877.",
   "kind": "plaque",
   "dx": -0.475,
   "plaque": "dingle_tom_crean",
   "title": "Tom Crean"
  },
  {
   "x": 55,
   "y": 15,
   "plaque": "dingle_crean_walk",
   "title": "Crean's Long Walk",
   "text": "In 1912, in Antarctica, Tom Crean walked about 35 miles alone across the ice, with little food, to fetch help for a dying shipmate. The man lived, and Crean got the Albert Medal. Back home he opened a pub at Annascaul and called it the South Pole Inn."
  },
  {
   "x": 39,
   "y": 33,
   "plaque": "dingle_ogham",
   "title": "Ogham Stones",
   "text": "County Kerry has more ogham stones than anywhere, and this peninsula is thick with them. Ogham is the oldest writing in Irish: groups of notches cut along the edge of a stone, mostly from the 5th and 6th centuries. Most just give a name and a family, like a very heavy business card."
  },
  {
   "x": 36,
   "y": 25,
   "plaque": "dingle_wren_day",
   "title": "Wren Day",
   "text": "On St Stephen's Day, 26 December, Dingle's wren boys take to the streets in straw masks and costumes with drums, whistles and a great deal of noise. In older days they carried a wren. Now nobody is chased but the quiet."
  },
  {
   "x": 52,
   "y": 28,
   "plaque": "dingle_ryans_daughter",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "Ryan's Daughter",
   "text": "In 1969 David Lean filmed Ryan's Daughter on the peninsula, building a whole village on the hill near Dunquin. The film crew stayed for a year, waiting for the right storms. Dingle has rarely had to wait long for a storm since."
  }
 ],
